***conformance tests***

# valid-file
Parsing the file named `valid.plain`, with `notes.md` in its template directory:
~~~
---
module: task-manager
func_id: add-task
---

> A comment before the first section.

***conformance tests***

# add-first-task
Adding a ⟨Task⟩ named "Buy milk" to an empty ⟨TaskList⟩ shows one ⟨Task⟩ named "Buy milk".
  - ⟨App⟩ prints `Added task 1: Buy milk`.

# add-without-name

given:
  - an empty ⟨TaskList⟩
when:
  - ⟨User⟩ runs `todo add`
then:
  - stderr is `Error: name is required`
> A comment inside a body is dropped.
  - exit status is 1


# gherkin-shape
Scenario: adding a task with a note
  Given an empty ⟨TaskList⟩
  When ⟨User⟩ adds "Buy milk"

  Then ⟨TaskList⟩ shows it

# links-the-notes
The output matches [the notes](notes.md).

***acceptance tests***

# add-then-list
- ⟨User⟩ adds a ⟨Task⟩ named "Buy milk".
- `todo list` shows `1 Buy milk`.
~~~
gives the name `valid.plain`, module `task-manager`, func-id `add-task`, and:
- conformance `add-first-task` with its two lines as text, `add-without-name` with the seven lines from `given:` to `  - exit status is 1` without the comment, `gherkin-shape` with its five lines, the empty line kept, and `links-the-notes` with linked_resources [{text `the notes`, target `notes.md`}];
- acceptance `add-then-list` with its two lines;
- the heading lines 10, 14, 26, 33 and 38, conformance first;
- every other scenario with empty linked_resources and no `code_variables` key.

# empty-sections
A file whose two sections are empty, a file whose two section lines follow the frontmatter on adjacent lines, and a file whose conformance section holds only the comment `> add conformance test scenarios here` each give no scenario.

# liquid-and-code-variables
With `shape.plain` holding `  - exit status is {% raw %}{{ status | code_variable }}{% endraw %}` in a template directory, the scenario `works` whose text is `It works.` and the line `{% raw %}{% include "shape.plain", status: "0" %}{% endraw %}` becomes {id `works`, text `It works.` and the line `  - exit status is ` with the placeholder of `status`, linked_resources [], code_variables [{name `status`, value `0`}]}.

***acceptance tests***

# liquid-error-names-the-file
The file `f.plain` whose scenario text holds an unclosed `{% raw %}{{ works{% endraw %}` raises `PlainSyntaxError` holding `Invalid Liquid at f.plain`.
